  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nShowers and areas of embedded thunderstorms continue this afternoon \nahead of an approaching upper level vorticity max. Efficient \nrainfalls rates have been observed (1+ inches per hour) in the high \nPWAT environment (~2 inches) over the Ohio Valley. Locations that \nreceive repeated rounds of convection may experience upwards of 1-2+ \ninches of rain this afternoon and evening. This heavy rain potential \nsupports an isolated flash flood risk. Locations highlighted by the \nFlood Watch have the highest probabilities of receiving narrow swaths\nof very heavy rain. Showers and storms end tonight when forcing \nmoves east of the area.\n\nOn Sunday, a cold front moves in from the northwest. Warm and humid \nconditions are expected ahead of the front. Flow and shear increase \nas the front approaches from the north during the day. There may end\nup being an overlap of ample shear and instability Sunday \nafternoon/evening to support isolated to scattered showers and \nstorms, some of which could become severe (SPC marginal risk).\nCurrently, there storm coverage is not forecast to be all that\nwidespread.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nSurface high pressure moves northeast of the Ohio Valley to start \nthe work week. Developing southerly flow behind the high leads to \nhot and humid conditions to returning Tuesday into Wednesday when a \nwarm front lifts through the region. Shower and storms chances \nincrease during the second half the week as a front sags into the \narea from the north. The location of the front will likely impact\ntemperatures and rain chances with areas north of the front possibly\nturning a bit cooler than areas to south.",
